pg:CO1 a ipo:LearningObjective, owl:NamedIndividual ; rdfs:label "Create a database file or an in-memory database, create tables with typed columns and constraints, and read the schema back with sqlite_schema and PRAGMA table_info"@en ;
    skos:definition "The objective is met when the reader can create a database file or an in-memory database, create tables with typed columns and constraints, and read the schema back with sqlite_schema and PRAGMA table_info, at the apply level."@en ; dcterms:source <https://automatetheboringstuff.com/3e/chapter16.html> ;
    ld:bloomLevel ld:Apply  .
pg:CO2 a ipo:LearningObjective, owl:NamedIndividual ; rdfs:label "Write and read rows with INSERT, SELECT, UPDATE and DELETE using placeholders, WHERE, ORDER BY and LIMIT, and predict how many rows a statement changes or returns"@en ;
    skos:definition "The objective is met when the reader can write and read rows with INSERT, SELECT, UPDATE and DELETE using placeholders, WHERE, ORDER BY and LIMIT, and predict how many rows a statement changes or returns, at the apply level."@en ; dcterms:source <https://automatetheboringstuff.com/3e/chapter16.html> ;
    ld:bloomLevel ld:Apply  .
pg:CO3 a ipo:LearningObjective, owl:NamedIndividual ; rdfs:label "Group statements in a transaction and predict what commit, rollback, the connection's transaction mode and close keep or undo"@en ;
    skos:definition "The objective is met when the reader can group statements in a transaction and predict what commit, rollback, the connection's transaction mode and close keep or undo, at the apply level."@en ; dcterms:source <https://automatetheboringstuff.com/3e/chapter16.html> ;
    ld:bloomLevel ld:Apply  .
pg:CO4 a ipo:LearningObjective, owl:NamedIndividual ; rdfs:label "Link tables with foreign keys and joins, and explain why a foreign key that names rowid fails and what enforcement needs"@en ;
    skos:definition "The objective is met when the reader can link tables with foreign keys and joins, and explain why a foreign key that names rowid fails and what enforcement needs, at the analyze level."@en ; dcterms:source <https://automatetheboringstuff.com/3e/chapter16.html> ;
    ld:bloomLevel ld:Analyze  .
pg:CO5 a ipo:LearningObjective, owl:NamedIndividual ; rdfs:label "Copy a database with backup and iterdump, and judge what SQLite's design (one file, one writer, rowid, in memory) means for a program"@en ;
    skos:definition "The objective is met when the reader can copy a database with backup and iterdump, and judge what SQLite's design (one file, one writer, rowid, in memory) means for a program, at the analyze level."@en ; dcterms:source <https://automatetheboringstuff.com/3e/chapter16.html> ;
    ld:bloomLevel ld:Analyze  .
pg:Q1 a ld:AssessmentItem, owl:NamedIndividual ; rdfs:label "A table cats holds three rows. After UPDATE cats SET fur = 'gray' (no WHERE clause), how many rows did the statement change?"@en ; ld:hasOption pg:Q1_O0, pg:Q1_O1, pg:Q1_O2 ; ld:hasCorrectOption pg:Q1_O1 ; ld:cognitiveLevel ld:Apply ; ld:assessesObjective pg:CO2 ;
    ld:distractorRationale "A statement without WHERE is not limited to one row." ;
    ld:distractorRationale "Zero rows are changed only when a WHERE clause matches nothing." .
pg:Q1_O0 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "1" .
pg:Q1_O1 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "3" .
pg:Q1_O2 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "0" .
pg:Q2 a ld:AssessmentItem, owl:NamedIndividual ; rdfs:label "What does SELECT * FROM cats leave out that SELECT rowid, * FROM cats shows?"@en ; ld:hasOption pg:Q2_O0, pg:Q2_O1, pg:Q2_O2 ; ld:hasCorrectOption pg:Q2_O1 ; ld:cognitiveLevel ld:Understand ; ld:assessesObjective pg:CO1 ;
    ld:distractorRationale "* means every declared column." ;
    ld:distractorRationale "Rows with equal values are still returned; the rowid is what tells them apart." .
pg:Q2_O0 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "The last column of the table" .
pg:Q2_O1 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "The hidden integer key of each row" .
pg:Q2_O2 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "Rows whose values are all equal" .
pg:Q3 a ld:AssessmentItem, owl:NamedIndividual ; rdfs:label "In an ordinary table (no STRICT) a column is declared INTEGER. After INSERT INTO t VALUES ('42'), ('Hello'), what does SELECT n, typeof(n) FROM t give?"@en ; ld:hasOption pg:Q3_O0, pg:Q3_O1, pg:Q3_O2 ; ld:hasCorrectOption pg:Q3_O0 ; ld:cognitiveLevel ld:Apply ; ld:assessesObjective pg:CO1 ;
    ld:distractorRationale "Only a STRICT table refuses 'Hello'." ;
    ld:distractorRationale "The text '42' can be converted losslessly, so it is stored as the integer 42." .
pg:Q3_O0 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "[(42, 'integer'), ('Hello', 'text')]" .
pg:Q3_O1 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "[(42, 'integer')] and an error for 'Hello'" .
pg:Q3_O2 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "[('42', 'text'), ('Hello', 'text')]" .
pg:Q4 a ld:AssessmentItem, owl:NamedIndividual ; rdfs:label "A query is built as 'SELECT * FROM cats WHERE name = '' + e + ''' and, separately, as SELECT * FROM cats WHERE name = ? with (e,), where e is x' OR '1'='1 and the table has 3 cats. How many rows do they return?"@en ; ld:hasOption pg:Q4_O0, pg:Q4_O1, pg:Q4_O2 ; ld:hasCorrectOption pg:Q4_O0 ; ld:cognitiveLevel ld:Apply ; ld:assessesObjective pg:CO2 ;
    ld:distractorRationale "The first query's text was changed by the input, so it matches every row." ;
    ld:distractorRationale "The placeholder binds the whole input as one value, so nothing matches." .
pg:Q4_O0 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "3 and 0" .
pg:Q4_O1 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "0 and 0" .
pg:Q4_O2 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "3 and 3" .
pg:Q5 a ld:AssessmentItem, owl:NamedIndividual ; rdfs:label "A connection is made with isolation_level=None. One INSERT is run with no BEGIN, then conn.rollback() is called. Is the new row still in the table?"@en ; ld:hasOption pg:Q5_O0, pg:Q5_O1, pg:Q5_O2 ; ld:hasCorrectOption pg:Q5_O0 ; ld:cognitiveLevel ld:Apply ; ld:assessesObjective pg:CO3 ;
    ld:distractorRationale "rollback() undoes only an open transaction, and there is none." ;
    ld:distractorRationale "Nothing empties a table here." .
pg:Q5_O0 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "Yes, the INSERT was its own transaction and was already kept" .
pg:Q5_O1 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "No, rollback() undoes the last statement" .
pg:Q5_O2 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "No, the table is emptied" .
pg:Q6 a ld:AssessmentItem, owl:NamedIndividual ; rdfs:label "A file database is opened with sqlite3.connect(path) and its default settings. A row is inserted, and close() is called without commit(). What does a new connection to the file see for that row?"@en ; ld:hasOption pg:Q6_O0, pg:Q6_O1, pg:Q6_O2 ; ld:hasCorrectOption pg:Q6_O1 ; ld:cognitiveLevel ld:Analyze ; ld:assessesObjective pg:CO3 ;
    ld:distractorRationale "In the default mode close() does not commit." ;
    ld:distractorRationale "The file is fine; the transaction was simply never committed." .
pg:Q6_O0 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "The row, because close() saves" .
pg:Q6_O1 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "Nothing: the uncommitted change was discarded" .
pg:Q6_O2 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "An error, because the file is damaged" .
pg:Q7 a ld:AssessmentItem, owl:NamedIndividual ; rdfs:label "The chapter declares FOREIGN KEY(cat_id) REFERENCES cats(rowid). With PRAGMA foreign_keys = ON, what happens when a row is inserted into the vaccinations table?"@en ; ld:hasOption pg:Q7_O0, pg:Q7_O1, pg:Q7_O2 ; ld:hasCorrectOption pg:Q7_O1 ; ld:cognitiveLevel ld:Analyze ; ld:assessesObjective pg:CO4 ;
    ld:distractorRationale "That is what a declared parent key would do, but the rowid cannot be one." ;
    ld:distractorRationale "Nothing is copied; the constraint cannot even be checked." .
pg:Q7_O0 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "It is accepted when the cat exists and refused when it does not" .
pg:Q7_O1 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "OperationalError: foreign key mismatch, for every insert" .
pg:Q7_O2 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "It is accepted and the rowid is copied" .
pg:Q8 a ld:AssessmentItem, owl:NamedIndividual ; rdfs:label "Tables cats and vaccinations are joined with cats LEFT JOIN vaccinations ON cats.id = vaccinations.cat_id WHERE vaccinations.vaccine IS NULL. Which cats does this return?"@en ; ld:hasOption pg:Q8_O0, pg:Q8_O1, pg:Q8_O2 ; ld:hasCorrectOption pg:Q8_O1 ; ld:cognitiveLevel ld:Apply ; ld:assessesObjective pg:CO4 ;
    ld:distractorRationale "That is what an inner join shows." ;
    ld:distractorRationale "That needs the tables the other way round." .
pg:Q8_O0 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "Cats with at least one vaccination" .
pg:Q8_O1 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "Cats with no vaccination at all" .
pg:Q8_O2 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "Vaccinations that point to no cat" .
pg:Q9 a ld:AssessmentItem, owl:NamedIndividual ; rdfs:label "A table without an INTEGER PRIMARY KEY holds rows with rowids 1, 2, 3. The row with rowid 3 is deleted and a new row is inserted. What rowid does the new row get?"@en ; ld:hasOption pg:Q9_O0, pg:Q9_O1, pg:Q9_O2 ; ld:hasCorrectOption pg:Q9_O1 ; ld:cognitiveLevel ld:Analyze ; ld:assessesObjective pg:CO1 ;
    ld:distractorRationale "Only AUTOINCREMENT prevents reuse." ;
    ld:distractorRationale "SQLite picks one more than the largest rowid in use, not the first gap." .
pg:Q9_O0 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "4, because rowids are never reused" .
pg:Q9_O1 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "3, the largest number in use plus one" .
pg:Q9_O2 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "1, the first free number" .
pg:Q10 a ld:AssessmentItem, owl:NamedIndividual ; rdfs:label "Two separate connections are opened with sqlite3.connect(':memory:'). The first creates a table t. What does a query on t through the second connection do?"@en ; ld:hasOption pg:Q10_O0, pg:Q10_O1, pg:Q10_O2 ; ld:hasCorrectOption pg:Q10_O1 ; ld:cognitiveLevel ld:Apply ; ld:assessesObjective pg:CO5 ;
    ld:distractorRationale "Each ':memory:' connection has its own private database." ;
    ld:distractorRationale "There is nothing to wait for; the databases are unrelated." .
pg:Q10_O0 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "Returns the rows of t" .
pg:Q10_O1 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "Raises OperationalError: no such table: t" .
pg:Q10_O2 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "Waits until the first connection closes" .
pg:Q11 a ld:AssessmentItem, owl:NamedIndividual ; rdfs:label "The cats table holds Zophie 5.6 kg, Miguel 6.2 kg and Toby 6.8 kg. What does SELECT name FROM cats ORDER BY weight_kg DESC LIMIT 2 return?"@en ; ld:hasOption pg:Q11_O0, pg:Q11_O1, pg:Q11_O2 ; ld:hasCorrectOption pg:Q11_O1 ; ld:cognitiveLevel ld:Apply ; ld:assessesObjective pg:CO2 ;
    ld:distractorRationale "ORDER BY weight_kg DESC starts with the largest." ;
    ld:distractorRationale "Zophie is the lightest of the three." .
pg:Q11_O0 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "[('Miguel',), ('Toby',)]" .
pg:Q11_O1 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "[('Toby',), ('Miguel',)]" .
pg:Q11_O2 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "[('Toby',), ('Zophie',)]" .
pg:Q12 a ld:AssessmentItem, owl:NamedIndividual ; rdfs:label "What limit of SQLite does the chapter name, and what rule in the SQLite documentation lies behind it?"@en ; ld:hasOption pg:Q12_O0, pg:Q12_O1, pg:Q12_O2 ; ld:hasCorrectOption pg:Q12_O1 ; ld:cognitiveLevel ld:Understand ; ld:assessesObjective pg:CO5 ;
    ld:distractorRationale "The chapter says SQLite handles gigabytes and even terabytes." ;
    ld:distractorRationale "Readers can work at the same time; the limit is on writing." .
pg:Q12_O0 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "Databases cannot exceed 1 GB; every table holds one writer's rows" .
pg:Q12_O1 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "It cannot efficiently handle hundreds of simultaneous writes; a database file has one writer at a time" .
pg:Q12_O2 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "It cannot read from two programs at once; files are locked for reading" .
pg:DatabaseIdea a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Database idea"@en ;
    skos:definition "Chapter 16 opens by setting a database against two things the reader already knows, the spreadsheet of chapters 14 and 15 and the plain text file, and against the other SQL databases the reader may have met (Sweigart, 2025). It calls SQLite a lightweight database included with Python, stored in a single file, and the most widely deployed database software (Sweigart, 2025)(SQLite documentation, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch16#S_DatabaseIdea> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-DatabaseIdea" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 1 .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:DatabaseIdea .
pg:SpreadsheetVersusTable a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Spreadsheet versus table"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter says a table can be thought of as a spreadsheet, a list of tuples whose rows are records and whose columns are the kinds of data, and that SQLite adds a rowid column to each table, a unique integer that does not change when other rows are deleted (Sweigart, 2025). The SQLite pages on rowid tables and on VACUUM and AUTOINCREMENT qualify that last claim (SQLite documentation, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch16#S_SpreadsheetVersusTable> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-SpreadsheetVersusTable" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 2 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:DatabaseIdea .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:SpreadsheetVersusTable .
pg:TableIsTupleList a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Table is tuple list"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter writes that the cats table is essentially [('Zophie', '2021-01-24', 'black', 5.6), ('Colin', '2016-12-24', 'siamese', 6.2), ...] (Sweigart, 2025). The DB-API says a fetch returns a sequence of rows, and the module's default row is a tuple (Python Software Foundation, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch16#S_TableIsTupleList> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-TableIsTupleList" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:SpreadsheetVersusTable .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:TableIsTupleList .
pg:RowidHidden a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Rowid hidden"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter says the star means all columns except rowid and that a query for rowid and name returns both (Sweigart, 2025). SQLite's page on rowid tables calls the rowid a unique, non-NULL, signed 64-bit integer that every such table has, and the page names the aliases rowid, oid and _rowid_ (SQLite documentation, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch16#S_RowidHidden> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-RowidHidden" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:SpreadsheetVersusTable .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:RowidHidden .
pg:RowidReuse a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Rowid reuse"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter says a record's primary key ID is unique and does not change, so that a cat keeps its identity through deletions and reordering (Sweigart, 2025). SQLite's page on AUTOINCREMENT says that without the keyword the automatic assignment may reuse the rowids of rows that were deleted, and that the keyword exists to prevent exactly that (SQLite documentation, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch16#S_RowidReuse> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-RowidReuse" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:SpreadsheetVersusTable .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:RowidReuse .
pg:RowidAfterVacuum a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Rowid after vacuum"@en ;
    skos:definition "SQLite's page on VACUUM says the command may change the ROWIDs of entries in any tables that do not have an explicit INTEGER PRIMARY KEY (SQLite documentation, 2026). The chapter never runs VACUUM, but its backup and dump sections copy a database, and the page on the backup API describes the copy as page for page (Sweigart, 2025)(SQLite documentation, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch16#S_RowidAfterVacuum> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-RowidAfterVacuum" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:SpreadsheetVersusTable .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:RowidAfterVacuum .
pg:IntegerPrimaryKeyAlias a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Integer primary key alias"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter's own tables do not declare a key and use the hidden rowid (Sweigart, 2025). SQLite's page on CREATE TABLE says a column of type INTEGER PRIMARY KEY becomes an alias for the rowid, and the page on AUTOINCREMENT says the keyword adds CPU, memory and disk overhead and should be avoided if not strictly needed (SQLite documentation, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch16#S_IntegerPrimaryKeyAlias> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-IntegerPrimaryKeyAlias" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:SpreadsheetVersusTable .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:IntegerPrimaryKeyAlias .
pg:SqliteVersusServers a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Sqlite versus servers"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter lists the differences: one file, few resources, no server, no installation, optional in-memory use, data types that are not strictly enforced, no GRANT or REVOKE, public domain, and the one disadvantage that it cannot efficiently handle hundreds or thousands of simultaneous writes (Sweigart, 2025). SQLite's own pages on distinctive features, on serverless use and on when to use it say the same and add limits (SQLite documentation, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch16#S_SqliteVersusServers> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-SqliteVersusServers" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 2 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:DatabaseIdea .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:SqliteVersusServers .
pg:SingleFileServerless a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Single file serverless"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter says a SQLite database lives in a single file that can be moved, copied or backed up like any other, and that the database is serverless, with no background application and no network connection (Sweigart, 2025). SQLite's page on serverless operation says the library reads and writes the file directly in the process of the application (SQLite documentation, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch16#S_SingleFileServerless> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-SingleFileServerless" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:SqliteVersusServers .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:SingleFileServerless .
pg:NoGrantRevoke a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "No grant revoke"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter says SQLite has no permission settings or user roles and no GRANT or REVOKE statements, unlike other SQL databases (Sweigart, 2025). SQLite's page on serverless operation says the process reads and writes the database files directly and that there is no intermediary server (SQLite documentation, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch16#S_NoGrantRevoke> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-NoGrantRevoke" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:SqliteVersusServers .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:NoGrantRevoke .
pg:LargeDataLimits a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Large data limits"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter says SQLite can reliably handle gigabytes or even terabytes of data (Sweigart, 2025). SQLite's page of limits says the database can grow to about 17.5 terabytes at the default page size and about 281 terabytes at the largest, (SQLite documentation, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch16#S_LargeDataLimits> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-LargeDataLimits" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:SqliteVersusServers .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:LargeDataLimits .
pg:FilesVersusDatabase a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Files versus database"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter says SQLite sells itself as a competitor to open() on text files, and to the JSON, XML and CSV files of chapter 18, and that its query language replaces code that would search lists and dictionaries by hand, as regular expressions replace hand-written pattern matching (Sweigart, 2025)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch16#S_FilesVersusDatabase> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-FilesVersusDatabase" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 2 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:DatabaseIdea .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:FilesVersusDatabase .
pg:QueryVersusPythonLoop a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Query versus python loop"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter's first paragraph asks for the fur color of all cats that weighed between 3 and 5 kilograms and were born before October 2023 and calls the job tricky with regular expressions (Sweigart, 2025). SQLite's expression page says x BETWEEN y AND z is logically equivalent to a pair of comparisons, x >= y AND x <= z (SQLite documentation, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch16#S_QueryVersusPythonLoop> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-QueryVersusPythonLoop" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:FilesVersusDatabase .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:QueryVersusPythonLoop .
pg:ConnectionAndSchema a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Connection and schema"@en ;
    skos:definition "Before any row exists, a program opens a Connection with sqlite3.connect(), creates tables with CREATE TABLE and chooses a type for each column; the chapter covers this in 'Creating Databases and Tables', 'Defining Data Types' and 'Listing Tables and Columns' (Sweigart, 2025). The sqlite3 module of the standard library is the DB-API 2.0 interface to the SQLite library (Python Software Foundation, 2026)(Lemburg, 1999)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch16#S_ConnectionAndSchema> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-ConnectionAndSchema" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 1 .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:ConnectionAndSchema .
pg:Connecting a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Connecting"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter says sqlite3.connect() takes a filename or a pathlib.Path, creates an empty database when the file does not exist, raises sqlite3.DatabaseError: file is not a database at the first query if the file is something else, and returns a Connection that the program closes with conn.close() or leaves to be closed at exit (Sweigart, 2025). The Python documentation adds that since 3.13 a Connection deleted without close() emits a ResourceWarning (Python Software Foundation, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch16#S_Connecting> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-Connecting" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 2 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:ConnectionAndSchema .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:Connecting .
pg:ConnectCreatesFile a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Connect creates file"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter says that if the filename does not belong to an existing SQLite database, the function creates a new file containing an empty database (Sweigart, 2025). SQLite's file format page describes a database file as an integer number of pages in size (SQLite documentation, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch16#S_ConnectCreatesFile> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-ConnectCreatesFile" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:Connecting .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:ConnectCreatesFile .
pg:NotADatabaseFile a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Not a database file"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter says Python raises sqlite3.DatabaseError: file is not a database once the program tries to execute queries on a file that is not a SQLite database (Sweigart, 2025). The DB-API places DatabaseError under Error as the parent of errors related to the database (Lemburg, 1999)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch16#S_NotADatabaseFile> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-NotADatabaseFile" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:Connecting .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:NotADatabaseFile .
pg:PathLikeAndUri a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Path like and uri"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter says the first argument is a string or a pathlib.Path (Sweigart, 2025). SQLite's page on URI filenames defines the mode parameter as ro, rw, rwc or memory, and the Python documentation says uri=True makes connect() interpret the string as a URI (SQLite documentation, 2026)(Python Software Foundation, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch16#S_PathLikeAndUri> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-PathLikeAndUri" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:Connecting .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:PathLikeAndUri .
pg:CloseAndWarnings a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Close and warnings"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter says the program closes the connection automatically when it terminates (Sweigart, 2025). The Python documentation says the connection used as a context manager commits or rolls back and does not close, recommends contextlib.closing for closing, and says that since 3.13 a ResourceWarning is emitted if close() is not called before the object is deleted (Python Software Foundation, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch16#S_CloseAndWarnings> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-CloseAndWarnings" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:Connecting .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:CloseAndWarnings .
pg:TypingRules a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Typing rules"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter says SQLite has data types, but is not strict about them: it stores 'Hello' in an INTEGER column, converts '42' to 42 by type affinity, and offers the STRICT keyword to enforce types, which it recommends; it also says there is no Boolean and no date type, so Booleans are INTEGER 0 and 1 and dates are TEXT in the formats of Table 16-1 (Sweigart, 2025). SQLite's page on datatypes says its dynamic typing associates the type with the value and not with the column (SQLite documentation, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch16#S_TypingRules> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-TypingRules" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 2 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:ConnectionAndSchema .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:TypingRules .
pg:StorageClasses a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Storage classes"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter's list names NULL, INT or INTEGER, REAL, TEXT and BLOB and pairs each with a Python type, None, int, float, str and bytes (Sweigart, 2025). SQLite's page on datatypes calls these five names the storage classes and says the type is a property of the value (SQLite documentation, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch16#S_StorageClasses> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-StorageClasses" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:TypingRules .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:StorageClasses .
pg:SixTypesMiscount a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Six types miscount"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter's sentence is 'There are six data types in SQLite' and its list has the entries NULL, INT or INTEGER, REAL, TEXT and BLOB (Sweigart, 2025). SQLite's page on STRICT tables lists the allowed column types as INT, INTEGER, REAL, TEXT, BLOB and ANY, and says no other datatype names are allowed (SQLite documentation, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch16#S_SixTypesMiscount> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-SixTypesMiscount" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:TypingRules .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:SixTypesMiscount .
pg:TypeAffinity a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Type affinity"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter says SQLite casts data to the column's type if possible, a feature called type affinity, and that 'Hello' in an INTEGER column is stored as 'Hello' without error (Sweigart, 2025). SQLite's page on datatypes gives five affinities, TEXT, NUMERIC, INTEGER, REAL and BLOB, and the rules by which the declared type of a column picks one (SQLite documentation, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch16#S_TypeAffinity> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-TypeAffinity" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:TypingRules .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:TypeAffinity .
pg:StrictMode a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Strict mode"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter says strict mode makes every column take a data type, raises IntegrityError for data of the wrong type, still casts where it can, and that the author highly recommends it (Sweigart, 2025). SQLite's page on STRICT tables says the same and adds that the freedom to specify a column without a datatype is removed and that the ANY type can host any type of data (SQLite documentation, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch16#S_StrictMode> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-StrictMode" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:TypingRules .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:StrictMode .
pg:BooleanAndDates a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Boolean and dates"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter says Booleans are INTEGER 1 and 0 and dates are TEXT in the formats of Table 16-1, that comparisons of dates work as strings in YYYY-MM-DD form (Sweigart, 2025). SQLite's page on date and time functions lists those formats and the seven functions that read them (SQLite documentation, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch16#S_BooleanAndDates> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-BooleanAndDates" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:TypingRules .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:BooleanAndDates .
pg:PythonValueMapping a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Python value mapping"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter pairs the SQLite types with Python's None, int, float, str and bytes and does not mention anything else (Sweigart, 2025). The Python documentation lists the same five conversions in its table of SQLite and Python types and says other types need an adapter (Python Software Foundation, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch16#S_PythonValueMapping> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-PythonValueMapping" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:TypingRules .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:PythonValueMapping .
pg:SchemaIntrospection a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Schema introspection"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter creates a table with CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS, lists the tables by selecting from sqlite_schema and lists the columns with PRAGMA TABLE_INFO(cats), and explains the six fields of each tuple (Sweigart, 2025). SQLite's pages on CREATE TABLE and on PRAGMA statements describe them, and the file format page lists sqlite_master as an alias of sqlite_schema (SQLite documentation, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch16#S_SchemaIntrospection> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-SchemaIntrospection" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 2 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:ConnectionAndSchema .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:SchemaIntrospection .
pg:CreateIfNotExists a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Create if not exists"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter says the statement raises table cats already exists without IF NOT EXISTS, that including it avoids the exception and that you will almost always want it; it also says table and column names are case insensitive (Sweigart, 2025). SQLite's page on CREATE TABLE says that with IF NOT EXISTS the command is a no-op if a table of that name exists (SQLite documentation, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch16#S_CreateIfNotExists> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-CreateIfNotExists" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:SchemaIntrospection .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:CreateIfNotExists .
pg:SchemaTable a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Schema table"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter says all SQLite databases have a table named sqlite_schema that lists metadata, including all tables, that it is not itself listed and that the reader should never modify it (Sweigart, 2025). SQLite's file format page says the schema table can also be called by the aliases sqlite_master, sqlite_temp_schema and sqlite_temp_master (SQLite documentation, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch16#S_SchemaTable> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-SchemaTable" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:SchemaIntrospection .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:SchemaTable .
pg:TableInfo a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Table info"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter explains the tuple (1, 'birthdate', 'TEXT', 0, None, 0) field by field, as position, name, type, whether NOT NULL, default value and whether a primary key (Sweigart, 2025). SQLite's page on pragmas says table_info returns one row for each normal column in the named table and lists the columns of the result (SQLite documentation, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch16#S_TableInfo> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-TableInfo" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:SchemaIntrospection .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:TableInfo .
pg:VersionsAndBuilds a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Versions and builds"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter says STRICT was added in SQLite 3.37.0, 'which is used by Python 3.11 and later', and tells the reader to print sqlite3.sqlite_version, which 'will look something like' 3.xx.xx (Sweigart, 2025). SQLite's release log dates 3.37.0 at 2021-11-27, and Python's configure page says a build of Python 3.14 needs SQLite 3.15.2 or newer (SQLite documentation, 2026)(Python Software Foundation, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch16#S_VersionsAndBuilds> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-VersionsAndBuilds" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 2 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:ConnectionAndSchema .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:VersionsAndBuilds .
pg:SqliteVersion a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Sqlite version"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter prints sqlite3.sqlite_version as '3.xx.xx' and in the shell session as SQLite version 3.xx.xx, a placeholder (Sweigart, 2025). The Python documentation lists sqlite_version and sqlite_version_info as the version of the SQLite library at run time, and the notes of 3.14 list the removal of the old version attributes (Python Software Foundation, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch16#S_SqliteVersion> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-SqliteVersion" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:VersionsAndBuilds .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:SqliteVersion .
pg:StrictVersionClaim a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Strict version claim"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter's sentence ties STRICT to 3.37.0 and says that this is the version used by Python 3.11 and later, and that earlier versions report a syntax error (Sweigart, 2025). SQLite's release log for 3.37.0 gives 2021-11-27 and says STRICT tables were introduced there (SQLite documentation, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch16#S_StrictVersionClaim> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-StrictVersionClaim" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:VersionsAndBuilds .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:StrictVersionClaim .
pg:CrudOperations a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Crud operations"@en ;
    skos:definition "CRUD, the chapter says, is the four basic operations of a database, creating, reading, updating and deleting, which SQLite carries out with INSERT, SELECT, UPDATE and DELETE; most applications and social media sites are fancy user interfaces for a CRUD database (Sweigart, 2025)(Wikipedia, 2026). The chapter spends most of its pages here, with the cats of sweigartcats.db as data."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch16#S_CrudOperations> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-CrudOperations" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 1 .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:CrudOperations .
pg:Writing a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Writing"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter inserts one cat with INSERT INTO cats VALUES (...), says the parentheses are mandatory, updates with UPDATE ... SET ..."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch16#S_Writing> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-Writing" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 2 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:CrudOperations .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:Writing .
pg:InsertColumnList a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Insert column list"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter's INSERT lists only values, in the order of the table's columns, and says the parentheses are mandatory (Sweigart, 2025). SQLite's page on INSERT says that table columns that do not appear in the column list are populated with the default column value or with NULL (SQLite documentation, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch16#S_InsertColumnList> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-InsertColumnList" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:Writing .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:InsertColumnList .
pg:InsertMany a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Insert many"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter says an INSERT begins a transaction and that a SQLite query will either completely insert data or not at all, and its connections use isolation_level=None, which it says is autocommit mode (Sweigart, 2025). The Python documentation says executemany runs one statement against a sequence of parameters, and that isolation_level None disables the implicit opening of transactions (Python Software Foundation, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch16#S_InsertMany> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-InsertMany" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:Writing .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:InsertMany .
pg:UpdateDeleteWhere a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Update delete where"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter warns that UPDATE ... WHERE name = 'Zophie' applies to every cat of that name, that forgetting the WHERE clause changes every row, and that WHERE 1 marks a deliberate change of all rows; the same holds for DELETE (Sweigart, 2025)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch16#S_UpdateDeleteWhere> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-UpdateDeleteWhere" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:Writing .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:UpdateDeleteWhere .
pg:ReturningAndLastrowid a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Returning and lastrowid"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter finds a new cat's rowid by a separate SELECT of rowid, star WHERE name = 'Mango' (Sweigart, 2025). SQLite's page on RETURNING says the clause can appear at the end of top-level DELETE, INSERT and UPDATE statements and returns the rows they changed, and the Python documentation describes lastrowid as the row id of the last inserted row (SQLite documentation, 2026)(Python Software Foundation, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch16#S_ReturningAndLastrowid> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-ReturningAndLastrowid" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:Writing .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:ReturningAndLastrowid .
pg:Parameters a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Parameters"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter tells the reader to use the question mark whenever a variable goes into a query, shows an f-string insert as the wrong way and a list of values as the right way, and says SQL injection is beyond the book and unlikely if the program does not accept data from strangers on the internet (Sweigart, 2025). OWASP names prepared statements with parameterised queries as its first defence, and MITRE lists injection of SQL as the weakness CWE-89 (OWASP, 2026)(MITRE, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch16#S_Parameters> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-Parameters" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 2 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:CrudOperations .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:Parameters .
pg:QmarkPlaceholders a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Qmark placeholders"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter writes conn.execute('INSERT INTO cats VALUES (?, ?, ?, ?)', [cat_name, cat_bday, fur_color, cat_weight]) and says execute replaces the question marks after making sure the values cannot cause an injection (Sweigart, 2025). The Python documentation lists qmark as the style of the module and describes the second argument as a sequence or a mapping (Python Software Foundation, 2026)(Lemburg, 1999)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch16#S_QmarkPlaceholders> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-QmarkPlaceholders" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:Parameters .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:QmarkPlaceholders .
pg:NamedPlaceholders a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Named placeholders"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter shows only the question mark (Sweigart, 2025). The Python documentation describes the named style, :name with a dictionary, as an alternative, and the notes of 3.12 say that a sequence of parameters used with named placeholders gives a DeprecationWarning and will raise a ProgrammingError from 3.14 (Python Software Foundation, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch16#S_NamedPlaceholders> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-NamedPlaceholders" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:Parameters .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:NamedPlaceholders .
pg:SqlInjection a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Sql injection"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter calls injection beyond the scope of the book and mostly not an issue unless the program accepts data from strangers on the internet, and still advises the question mark (Sweigart, 2025). OWASP's cheat sheet makes parameterised queries the first defence, and MITRE's CWE-89 lists the weakness; xkcd's strip of 2007 made it a joke (OWASP, 2026)(MITRE, 2026)(Munroe, 2007)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch16#S_SqlInjection> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-SqlInjection" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:Parameters .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:SqlInjection .
pg:Reading a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Reading"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter reads with SELECT and fetchall, loops over the cursor, filters with WHERE and its operators, matches with LIKE and GLOB, sorts with ORDER BY and limits with LIMIT, in 'Reading Data from the Database' (Sweigart, 2025). SQLite's pages on SELECT and on expressions define them (SQLite documentation, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch16#S_Reading> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-Reading" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 2 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:CrudOperations .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:Reading .
pg:FetchMethods a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Fetch methods"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter says execute returns a Cursor, that fetchall returns a list of tuples in the order of the table's columns, and that a for loop can iterate over the rows without calling fetchall (Sweigart, 2025). The Python documentation describes fetchone, fetchmany with a size, fetchall, and the description attribute (Python Software Foundation, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch16#S_FetchMethods> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-FetchMethods" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:Reading .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:FetchMethods .
pg:WhereOperators a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Where operators"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter says SQLite uses = for equality where Python uses ==, and lists =, !=, <, >, <=, >=, AND, OR and NOT (Sweigart, 2025). SQLite's expression page lists = and == and <> and != in the same precedence level, and BETWEEN, IN and IS as further operators (SQLite documentation, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch16#S_WhereOperators> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-WhereOperators" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:Reading .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:WhereOperators .
pg:DoubleQuotedStrings a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Double quoted strings"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter writes every text value in double quotes inside single-quoted Python strings, and says the module uses either quote for TEXT values (Sweigart, 2025). SQLite's page of quirks says the SQL standard requires double quotes around identifiers and single quotes around strings, that SQLite accepts a double-quoted string literal as a misfeature, and its compile options page names SQLITE_DQS=0 as the setting that disables it (SQLite documentation, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch16#S_DoubleQuotedStrings> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-DoubleQuotedStrings" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:Reading .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:DoubleQuotedStrings .
pg:LikeAndGlob a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Like and glob"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter says LIKE does a case-insensitive match with % as the wildcard and GLOB a case-sensitive one with *, and uses them on cat names (Sweigart, 2025). SQLite's expression page says LIKE and GLOB differ in case handling, that SQLite only understands upper and lower case for ASCII characters by default, and describes ESCAPE for a literal percent sign (SQLite documentation, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch16#S_LikeAndGlob> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-LikeAndGlob" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:Reading .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:LikeAndGlob .
pg:OrderByLimit a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Order by limit"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter orders by fur and then birthdate, uses ASC and DESC, shows a [:3] slice as inefficient and LIMIT as faster, and says the clauses go in the order WHERE, ORDER BY, LIMIT (Sweigart, 2025). SQLite's SELECT page defines the same order and adds OFFSET (SQLite documentation, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch16#S_OrderByLimit> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-OrderByLimit" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:Reading .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:OrderByLimit .
pg:NullComparison a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Null comparison"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter says NOT NULL on a column means the Python None cannot be stored there, and never compares with NULL (Sweigart, 2025). SQLite's expression page lists IS and IS NOT beside = in its operator table, and its datatype page treats NULL as a storage class of its own (SQLite documentation, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch16#S_NullComparison> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-NullComparison" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:Reading .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:NullComparison .
pg:RowFactory a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Row factory"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter reads columns by tuple index, index 0 for the name and index 1 for the birthdate (Sweigart, 2025). The Python documentation describes sqlite3.Row as a highly optimised row factory with name-based access, and its how-to guide on row factories shows a dict factory (Python Software Foundation, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch16#S_RowFactory> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-RowFactory" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:Reading .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:RowFactory .
pg:Speed a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Speed"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter says an index is a data structure that organises a column, that it makes queries with WHERE on that column faster at the cost of storage and slower inserts and updates, that index names are global, that a database of a few thousand records can ignore them, and shows CREATE INDEX, a listing from sqlite_schema and DROP INDEX (Sweigart, 2025). SQLite's pages on CREATE INDEX and on the query planner define them (SQLite documentation, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch16#S_Speed> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-Speed" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 2 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:CrudOperations .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:Speed .
pg:CreateIndexPlan a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Create index plan"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter creates idx_name and idx_birthdate, says index names are global, and lists the indexes twice, first as idx_name and idx_birthdate and later, before DROP INDEX, as idx_birthdate and idx_name (Sweigart, 2025). SQLite's page on EXPLAIN QUERY PLAN describes SCAN as a read of every row and SEARCH as the use of an index (SQLite documentation, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch16#S_CreateIndexPlan> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-CreateIndexPlan" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:Speed .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:CreateIndexPlan .
pg:LikeAndIndex a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Like and index"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter motivates the index with a search on names and then searches with LIKE patterns (Sweigart, 2025). SQLite's optimiser page says a LIKE term can be used with an index only if the column is indexed with the NOCASE collating sequence while case_sensitive_like is off, and a GLOB term only with the BINARY sequence (SQLite documentation, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch16#S_LikeAndIndex> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-LikeAndIndex" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:Speed .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:LikeAndIndex .
pg:TransactionControl a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Transaction control"@en ;
    skos:definition "A transaction is, in the chapter's words, a unit of work in a database; an INSERT begins one, and a program can run BEGIN and then undo everything with conn.rollback() or keep it with conn.commit() (Sweigart, 2025). The chapter adds the ACID test, that transactions are atomic, consistent, isolated and durable, and says SQLite is ACID-compliant and has passed tests that simulate losing power (Sweigart, 2025)(SQLite documentation, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch16#S_TransactionControl> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-TransactionControl" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 1 .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:TransactionControl .
pg:Boundaries a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Boundaries"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter connects with isolation_level=None, which it says is autocommit mode that relieves the reader of calling commit() after each execute(), and then starts a transaction by hand with the query BEGIN, ending it with commit() or rollback() (Sweigart, 2025). The Python documentation describes three modes: the legacy one controlled by isolation_level, the autocommit attribute added in 3.12 and the PEP 249 behaviour with autocommit False (Python Software Foundation, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch16#S_Boundaries> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-Boundaries" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 2 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:TransactionControl .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:Boundaries .
pg:BeginCommitRollback a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Begin commit rollback"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter shows BEGIN, two INSERTs of Socks and Fluffy and rollback() with the selects that find nothing, then the same with commit(), and says each execute() is its own transaction in autocommit mode (Sweigart, 2025). SQLite's transaction page says a transaction can be ended with COMMIT or ROLLBACK (SQLite documentation, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch16#S_BeginCommitRollback> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-BeginCommitRollback" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:Boundaries .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:BeginCommitRollback .
pg:LegacyAutocommit a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Legacy autocommit"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter always passes isolation_level=None and does not describe the default (Sweigart, 2025). The Python documentation says isolation_level may be DEFERRED (the default), EXCLUSIVE, IMMEDIATE or None, that the legacy mode opens a transaction implicitly before INSERT, UPDATE, DELETE and REPLACE, and that the attribute has no effect unless autocommit is LEGACY_TRANSACTION_CONTROL (Python Software Foundation, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch16#S_LegacyAutocommit> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-LegacyAutocommit" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:Boundaries .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:LegacyAutocommit .
pg:AutocommitAttribute a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Autocommit attribute"@en ;
    skos:definition "The notes of Python 3.12 add the autocommit attribute and the documentation calls autocommit=False the behaviour that PEP 249 asks for, and says that with autocommit True commit() and rollback() do nothing (Python Software Foundation, 2026)(Lemburg, 1999). The chapter predates the attribute and uses isolation_level=None, which is the legacy way of getting autocommit (Sweigart, 2025)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch16#S_AutocommitAttribute> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-AutocommitAttribute" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:Boundaries .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:AutocommitAttribute .
pg:ContextManagerTransaction a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Context manager transaction"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter does not use the with statement for connections (Sweigart, 2025). The Python documentation describes the connection as a context manager that commits if the block succeeds and rolls back if an exception is raised, and warns that it does not close the connection (Python Software Foundation, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch16#S_ContextManagerTransaction> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-ContextManagerTransaction" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:Boundaries .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:ContextManagerTransaction .
pg:ExecuteScriptCommit a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Execute script commit"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter does not cover executescript (Sweigart, 2025). The Python documentation says that in the legacy mode it first issues a COMMIT, and that this does not happen when autocommit is not LEGACY_TRANSACTION_CONTROL; it also describes it as the method for several statements in one string (Python Software Foundation, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch16#S_ExecuteScriptCommit> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-ExecuteScriptCommit" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:Boundaries .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:ExecuteScriptCommit .
pg:Guarantees a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Guarantees"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter defines ACID as atomic (completely or not at all), consistent (no constraint violated), isolated (one transaction does not affect another) and durable (committed results written to persistent storage), and says SQLite is ACID-compliant and has passed tests that simulate the loss of power (Sweigart, 2025). SQLite's own pages describe the same four guarantees and the tests (SQLite documentation, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch16#S_Guarantees> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-Guarantees" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 2 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:TransactionControl .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:Guarantees .
pg:AtomicStatement a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Atomic statement"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter says the transaction is carried out completely or not at all, and that a query will either completely insert data or not insert it at all (Sweigart, 2025). SQLite's page on atomic commit describes how it keeps an update from being half-applied, with a rollback journal (SQLite documentation, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch16#S_AtomicStatement> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-AtomicStatement" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:Guarantees .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:AtomicStatement .
pg:ConsistentConstraints a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Consistent constraints"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter's example of consistency is the NOT NULL rule for columns (Sweigart, 2025). SQLite's page on CREATE TABLE lists the constraints PRIMARY KEY, UNIQUE, CHECK, NOT NULL and the foreign key and says what happens on a violation, and the Python documentation puts all such failures under IntegrityError (SQLite documentation, 2026)(Python Software Foundation, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch16#S_ConsistentConstraints> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-ConsistentConstraints" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:Guarantees .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:ConsistentConstraints .
pg:IsolatedFromOthers a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Isolated from others"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter defines isolated as one transaction not affecting other transactions (Sweigart, 2025). SQLite's transaction page says that while a transaction is open other connections continue to see an historic snapshot of the database prior to the changes, and its transactional page says transactions are serializable (SQLite documentation, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch16#S_IsolatedFromOthers> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-IsolatedFromOthers" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:Guarantees .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:IsolatedFromOthers .
pg:DurableAfterReopen a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Durable after reopen"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter says durable means that committed results are written to persistent storage such as the hard drive, and that SQLite has even passed tests that simulate the loss of power in the middle of a transaction (Sweigart, 2025). SQLite's page on how it is tested says crash and power loss tests are done in simulation, because crash testing with real power failures is impractical (SQLite documentation, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch16#S_DurableAfterReopen> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-DurableAfterReopen" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:Guarantees .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:DurableAfterReopen .
pg:Concurrency a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Concurrency"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter names the one disadvantage of SQLite, that it cannot efficiently handle hundreds or thousands of simultaneous write operations, for instance from a social media web app, and says it handles simultaneous reads well (Sweigart, 2025). SQLite's page on file locking describes the locks, and its page on write-ahead logging describes the mode that changes the answer (SQLite documentation, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch16#S_Concurrency> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-Concurrency" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 2 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:TransactionControl .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:Concurrency .
pg:WriterLocked a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Writer locked"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter names simultaneous writes as the limit of SQLite without showing one (Sweigart, 2025). SQLite's page on locking describes SHARED, RESERVED and PENDING locks, of which only one connection can hold RESERVED, and the Python documentation sets the default timeout of connect() at 5.0 seconds (SQLite documentation, 2026)(Python Software Foundation, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch16#S_WriterLocked> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-WriterLocked" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:Concurrency .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:WriterLocked .
pg:JournalModeWal a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Journal mode wal"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter does not mention journal modes (Sweigart, 2025). SQLite's page on write-ahead logging says WAL provides more concurrency as readers do not block writers and a writer does not block readers, and that the mode is persistent (SQLite documentation, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch16#S_JournalModeWal> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-JournalModeWal" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:Concurrency .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:JournalModeWal .
pg:TableStructure a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Table structure"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter changes tables with ALTER TABLE and DROP TABLE, and links them with foreign keys and joins, using a second table of vaccinations that points back at the cats (Sweigart, 2025). Its last sentences say that foreign keys and joins have additional features outside the book (Sweigart, 2025)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch16#S_TableStructure> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-TableStructure" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 1 .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:TableStructure .
pg:Changing a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Changing"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter runs ALTER TABLE cats RENAME TO felines, RENAME COLUMN fur TO description, ADD COLUMN is_loved INTEGER DEFAULT 1 and DROP COLUMN is_loved, then DROP TABLE felines, and advises limiting how often the tables change because the queries of the program must change with them (Sweigart, 2025). SQLite's page on ALTER TABLE lists the same commands and the cases in which they are refused (SQLite documentation, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch16#S_Changing> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-Changing" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 2 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:TableStructure .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:Changing .
pg:RenameTableAndColumn a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Rename table and column"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter renames cats to felines and fur to description and shows the new names in sqlite_schema and TABLE_INFO (Sweigart, 2025). SQLite's page on ALTER TABLE says RENAME TO changes the name of the table, and RENAME COLUMN changes the column name in the table and in the indexes, triggers and views that refer to it (SQLite documentation, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch16#S_RenameTableAndColumn> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-RenameTableAndColumn" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:Changing .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:RenameTableAndColumn .
pg:AddColumn a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Add column"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter adds is_loved with a default of 1 and prints the first three rows with the new column (Sweigart, 2025). SQLite's page on ALTER TABLE says the new column may not have a PRIMARY KEY or UNIQUE constraint and, if it is NOT NULL, must have a default value other than NULL (SQLite documentation, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch16#S_AddColumn> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-AddColumn" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:Changing .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:AddColumn .
pg:DropColumn a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Drop column"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter drops is_loved, says any data stored in the deleted column is deleted and prints the columns before and after (Sweigart, 2025). SQLite's page on ALTER TABLE says DROP COLUMN only works if the column is not referenced by any other parts of the schema, is not a PRIMARY KEY and does not have a UNIQUE constraint (SQLite documentation, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch16#S_DropColumn> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-DropColumn" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:Changing .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:DropColumn .
pg:DropTable a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Drop table"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter drops felines and shows sqlite_schema empty (Sweigart, 2025). SQLite's page on DROP TABLE says the dropped table is completely removed from the database schema and the disk file, that it can not be recovered, and that all indices and triggers associated with it are also deleted (SQLite documentation, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch16#S_DropTable> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-DropTable" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:Changing .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:DropTable .
pg:Linking a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Linking"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter says the cats' vaccinations are a varying number of items and so belong in a second table whose rows refer back to the first, links them with a FOREIGN KEY(cat_id) REFERENCES cats(rowid), joins them with INNER JOIN ... ON cats.rowid = vaccinations.cat_id, and says foreign keys keep data consistent when PRAGMA foreign_keys = ON is run (Sweigart, 2025)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch16#S_Linking> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-Linking" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 2 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:TableStructure .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:Linking .
pg:SeparateTableDesign a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Separate table design"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter says a varying amount of data belongs as rows in a separate table that refer back to the main table, and not as columns named vaccination1, vaccination2 and so on, for the same reason as variables with numbers in their names (Sweigart, 2025). Codd's paper of 1970 introduced the foreign key, a domain of one relation that is not its primary key but whose elements are values of the primary key of another relation (Codd, 1970)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch16#S_SeparateTableDesign> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-SeparateTableDesign" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:Linking .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:SeparateTableDesign .
pg:InnerJoin a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Inner join"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter joins cats and vaccinations and prints rows that combine the columns of both, and says foreign keys also protect against orphaned vaccination records (Sweigart, 2025). SQLite's SELECT page defines the join operator and its ON constraint (SQLite documentation, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch16#S_InnerJoin> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-InnerJoin" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:Linking .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:InnerJoin .
pg:LeftJoin a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Left join"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter's practice program asks for all cats that lack the vaccines rabies, FeLV and FVRCP, and describes only the inner join (Sweigart, 2025). SQLite's SELECT page lists LEFT OUTER JOIN among its join operators beside INNER JOIN (SQLite documentation, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch16#S_LeftJoin> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-LeftJoin" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:Linking .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:LeftJoin .
pg:ForeignKeyMismatch a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Foreign key mismatch"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter declares the foreign key against the rowid of the parent, tells the reader to turn enforcement on with PRAGMA foreign_keys = ON, and says that a vaccination for a nonexistent cat then cannot be inserted (Sweigart, 2025). SQLite's page says the parent key must be a named column or columns in the parent table, not the rowid, and that the usual error is foreign key mismatch (SQLite documentation, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch16#S_ForeignKeyMismatch> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-ForeignKeyMismatch" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:Linking .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:ForeignKeyMismatch .
pg:ForeignKeyEnforcement a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Foreign key enforcement"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter says the safety features of foreign keys are disabled by default and are enabled by running the PRAGMA query after calling sqlite3.connect() (Sweigart, 2025). SQLite's pages say enforcement is off by default for backwards compatibility and must be enabled for each connection, and that the pragma is a no-op within a transaction (SQLite documentation, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch16#S_ForeignKeyEnforcement> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-ForeignKeyEnforcement" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:Linking .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:ForeignKeyEnforcement .
pg:ShippedDatabase a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Shipped database"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter's sessions use sweigartcats.db, which the reader downloads from the book's page, and the book's online materials hold it; the chapter says the author has about 300 cats (Sweigart, 2025). The file was taken from the zip of the materials, whose SHA-256 is recorded, and opened read-only (This study, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch16#S_ShippedDatabase> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-ShippedDatabase" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 2 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:TableStructure .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:ShippedDatabase .
pg:ShippedSchema a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Shipped schema"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter says the reader adds a second vaccinations table to the downloaded file, and prints Zophie as 'gray tabby' and Mango as rowid 23 (Sweigart, 2025). The online materials of the 3rd edition were downloaded from the book's page and the file inspected (DB Browser for SQLite, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch16#S_ShippedSchema> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-ShippedSchema" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:ShippedDatabase .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:ShippedSchema .
pg:ShippedPlantedErrors a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Shipped planted errors"@en ;
    skos:definition "The practice program asks for the cats without the vaccines rabies, FeLV and FVRCP and for the vaccines given before the cat's birthday, and says the database may contain errors (Sweigart, 2025). The answers are not printed in the chapter or in the materials."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch16#S_ShippedPlantedErrors> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-ShippedPlantedErrors" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:ShippedDatabase .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:ShippedPlantedErrors .
pg:CopiesToolsAndPractice a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Copies tools and practice"@en ;
    skos:definition "The last part of the chapter is about what surrounds a database: backing it up, keeping it in memory, copying it as text, working with it in the command-line tool and in graphical apps, and then the 16 practice questions and two practice programs (Sweigart, 2025). The chapter's story of a cat that walked on a keyboard and ran an UPDATE without WHERE twice is its argument for backups (Sweigart, 2025)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch16#S_CopiesToolsAndPractice> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-CopiesToolsAndPractice" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 1 .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:CopiesToolsAndPractice .
pg:Memory a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Memory"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter says an in-memory database, opened with the filename ':memory:', makes a large number of queries much faster, that it must be saved with backup() and is lost if the program crashes, and that the same method loads a file into memory; the same method also backs up a database that other programs are using, where copying the file would not be safe (Sweigart, 2025). SQLite's pages on in-memory databases and the backup API describe both (SQLite documentation, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch16#S_Memory> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-Memory" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 2 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:CopiesToolsAndPractice .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:Memory .
pg:InMemoryDatabase a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "In memory database"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter says to pass ':memory:' as the filename for an in-memory database and warns that it is lost if the program crashes (Sweigart, 2025). SQLite's page on in-memory databases says each connection to ':memory:' creates a new database, that a named in-memory database can be shared with the URI file:memdb1?mode=memory&cache=shared, and that an empty filename gives a temporary database (SQLite documentation, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch16#S_InMemoryDatabase> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-InMemoryDatabase" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:Memory .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:InMemoryDatabase .
pg:BackupMethod a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Backup method"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter says backup() safely backs up a database in between the other queries being run on it, that shutil.copy of the file is enough when no program uses the database, and shows file to file, memory to file and file to memory (Sweigart, 2025). The Python documentation says backup works even if the database is being accessed by other clients, takes pages, progress and sleep arguments, and was added in 3.7 (Python Software Foundation, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch16#S_BackupMethod> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-BackupMethod" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:Memory .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:BackupMethod .
pg:Dump a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Dump"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter says iterdump() returns an iterator of the SQL statements that re-create a database, writes it to sweigartcats-queries.txt line by line and prints a listing that starts with BEGIN TRANSACTION and ends with COMMIT, with the table name in double quotes, and says the text is almost certainly larger than the database (Sweigart, 2025). The Python documentation adds a filter argument in 3.13 (Python Software Foundation, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch16#S_Dump> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-Dump" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 2 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:CopiesToolsAndPractice .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:Dump .
pg:DumpMethod a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Dump method"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter writes each line of iterdump() to a file and prints the beginning and the end of the result (Sweigart, 2025). The Python documentation says iterdump returns an iterator to dump the database as SQL source code, similar to the .dump command of the shell, and that the filter is a LIKE pattern for the names of the objects (Python Software Foundation, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch16#S_DumpMethod> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-DumpMethod" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:Dump .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:DumpMethod .
pg:DumpShippedFails a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Dump shipped fails"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter dumps sweigartcats.db and prints a listing of cats only (Sweigart, 2025). Python 3.13 and 3.14 call PRAGMA foreign_key_check at the start of the dump and turn foreign keys off in the output if there are violations, which the source of the standard library's dump module shows (Python Software Foundation, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch16#S_DumpShippedFails> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-DumpShippedFails" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:Dump .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:DumpShippedFails .
pg:Tools a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Tools"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter says the sqlite3 command is run from a terminal as sqlite3 example.db, that statements end with a semicolon, shows the prompt sqlite> and the .tables and .headers commands, gives installation steps for Windows, macOS and Ubuntu, and names three free apps with a graphical interface: DB Browser for SQLite, SQLite Studio and DBeaver Community (Sweigart, 2025). SQLite's page on the shell documents it, and Python's sqlite3 module has had its own command-line interface since 3.12 (SQLite documentation, 2026)(Python Software Foundation, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch16#S_Tools> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-Tools" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 2 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:CopiesToolsAndPractice .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:Tools .
pg:SqliteCli a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Sqlite cli"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter shows the shell session with the prompt sqlite> and the output Zophie|2021-01-24|gray tabby|4.7, and says queries must end with a semicolon (Sweigart, 2025). SQLite's page on the shell documents the dot commands .tables, .headers and .dump, and its download page lists the tools bundle by platform (SQLite documentation, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch16#S_SqliteCli> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-SqliteCli" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:Tools .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:SqliteCli .
pg:CliInteractive a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Cli interactive"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter shows the banner SQLite version 3.xx.xx, the prompt sqlite>, the result in the form Zophie|2021-01-24|gray tabby|4.7 and a different result after .headers on, with a header line (Sweigart, 2025). SQLite's shell page describes the interactive session and the output modes (SQLite documentation, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch16#S_CliInteractive> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-CliInteractive" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:Tools .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:CliInteractive .
pg:PythonShell a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Python shell"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter tells the reader to install the sqlite3 command or use a graphical app (Sweigart, 2025). The Python documentation says the module can be invoked as a script with python -m sqlite3 to provide a simple SQLite shell, added in 3.12, with -h and -v options (Python Software Foundation, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch16#S_PythonShell> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-PythonShell" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:Tools .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:PythonShell .
pg:GuiApps a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Gui apps"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter names DB Browser for SQLite at sqlitebrowser.org, SQLite Studio at sqlitestudio.pl and DBeaver Community at dbeaver.io as free, open source apps on Windows, macOS and Linux, and says it is still worth learning the text-based syntax (Sweigart, 2025). The three project pages were opened on 2026-10-12."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch16#S_GuiApps> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-GuiApps" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:Tools .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:GuiApps .
pg:Practice a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Practice"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter ends with a summary, 16 practice questions and two practice programs: a Cat Vaccination Checker that lists the cats lacking the vaccines rabies, FeLV and FVRCP and finds vaccines given before the cat's birthday, and a Meal Ingredients Database that creates two tables, with a foreign key to meals (rowid), and prompts for meals and ingredients (Sweigart, 2025). The summary recommends Practical SQL, 2nd edition (Sweigart, 2025)(DBeaver, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch16#S_Practice> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-Practice" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 2 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:CopiesToolsAndPractice .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:Practice .
pg:VaccinationChecker a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Vaccination checker"@en ;
    skos:definition "The first practice program asks for all cats that don't have vaccines named 'rabies', 'FeLV' and 'FVRCP', and for a check of the database for vaccines administered before the cat's birthday (Sweigart, 2025). Neither the chapter nor the online materials print an answer."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch16#S_VaccinationChecker> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-VaccinationChecker" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:Practice .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:VaccinationChecker .
pg:MealIngredients a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Meal ingredients"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter gives the two CREATE TABLE statements, the input forms 'quit' and 'meal:ingredient1,ingredient2' and a sample run (Sweigart, 2025). SQLite's foreign key page says the parent key must be a named column and not the rowid, and the sample run in the chapter shows the second meal under rice without the indentation of the first (SQLite documentation, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch16#S_MealIngredients> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-MealIngredients" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:Practice .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:MealIngredients .
pg:PracticeQuestions a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Practice questions"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter's questions ask for a Connection object, a CREATE TABLE statement, autocommit, the difference between INTEGER and REAL, what strict mode adds, what * means, what CRUD and ACID stand for, the statements that add and delete rows, what happens without a WHERE clause in an UPDATE, what an index and a foreign key are, how to delete a table, the filename of an in-memory database and how to copy a database (Sweigart, 2025)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch16#S_PracticeQuestions> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-PracticeQuestions" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:Practice .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:PracticeQuestions .
